 and one of the  people in the audience  asked the one of the ministers from that  country  a simple question if you could had the  opportunity  to give advice to the president of the  united states who's coming in this  january coming january what would you  say  and this minister without batting an  eye from a totally foreign country  said act like a superpower  you you cannot  contribute to a leadership vacuum or it  gets filled by somebody with  views and interests and behavior  patterns  that are not in our interest the  countries around the world  that have anything approximating  a free economic system or a relatively  free political system  or any country that may not have a free  political or a free economic system  but benefits from a relatively stable  world not stable but  relatively stable they're frightened  when they see  a vacuum created because they know it's  going to get filled  and it struck me that that that minister  in that country said it exactly right  that doesn't mean go be policemen of the  world it doesn't mean go get invaded  engaged in wars and conflicts and  try to pretend and i would add that i  don't think it means  that we should take our template that  we've arrived at  in the year 2016 and think  that we should press it down on other  countries we shouldn't  the template we've arrived at today is  not what we had 50  years ago or 100 years ago it's not what  we're going to have in 50 years from now  we've we're evolving just like they do  these other countries have  they have different neighbors they have  different cultures they have different  languages  they have different political  circumstances and it's not for us to  to to beat our chess and make a judgment  that what we've got is right  but if you want a visible  a stark example of what works  and what doesn't work my office has got  a satellite photo  in fact it's in my book my my memoir  a satellite photo of the korean  peninsula  you've got this dmz in the middle  demilitarized zone  i've promised i won't use acronyms  i i've told people that this is an  acronym-free zone so i apologize  i apologize for saying dmz it's the  demolitorized zone  same people north and south same  language  same neighbors same history same culture  and you look at that satellite photo and  there's electricity and energy down  south  and in the north there's a pin prick of  light in the capital of pyang  they're starving up north and the south  korean  economy is the 13th largest economy on  the face of the earth  it is a stunning photograph  and anyone who can't figure out that a  freer political system  not necessarily our model today but  opting towards a freer political system  and a freer economic system  is so vastly better than an  authoritarian system  or a communist command economy  uh it it the photograph says it all  and it seems to me that it's important  that we  we get a little preachy  sometimes as a country um  if if if there's the world  and this is quote by our current  standard bad  not good not like us is what we really  mean when we say bad  we're judgmental and this is good and  some country is over here in the  in the bad half but they're moving in  this direction  towards freer political systems and free  or economic systems it seems to me  you want to encourage that  you don't want to say well you're not  perfect you're not just like we are  you have to be just like we are well  that's silly  different countries at different times  need different political systems  they may very well even need different  economic systems  so we have to be careful about thinking  that  that we've always been what we are now  or that will always be i mean  women didn't vote into that 1900s we  still had slaves in this country in the  1800s  and and we're still evolving and  changing  and who knows what will look like in  another 50 or 100 years  so we shouldn't expect others to be  exactly like we are  and we shouldn't preach to them and tell  them they'll  now if you show them the korean  peninsula from a satellite  at night an untrained  ape ought to see what works  and what doesn't work  [Applause]  secretary rumsfeld thank you very much  for your speech tonight  my question is i guess somewhat  strategic so i'll  press you and i know you're not  currently engaged but  as we look at our global situation in  manufacturing  and our movement of manufacturing  overseas  and i look at world war ii and our  ability to produce whatever we wanted to  produce  to meet our challenges facing us at that  point  what is your opinion relative to our  ability to  address that if the balloon goes up  again  well i don't even slightly doubt  but that the united states could  mobilize and produce the kinds of things  and  technologies and capabilities that we  would need  if in fact it became clear that we  needed them  there's something about things that are  gradual  where we don't address them and it takes  leadership and it takes brains and it  takes insight  and it takes some of the things that  shelling wrote in that forward  to figure out what's important  but in a crisis people get out of their  chairs  and they say my goodness the pendulum's  gone too far one way  so i'm willing to turn off the  television or stop doing what i'm doing  and invest time  pushing that pendulum back where it  belongs and and that's happened  every time we have  faced a crisis in this country  and it's been impressive now  is it a different world today yeah it is  it's a different world  we we are the lethality of weapons has  become increasingly lethal every year  the technologies that very few countries  in the world can create and develop  and design we tend to sell  off the shelf to anyone who wants them  they couldn't development most the  countries couldn't develop those things  but they could  buy them and use them to their advantage  which may or may not be  our best interest  and as i say maybe i'm just too much of  an optimist  but there is not a doubt in my mind  but that a leadership  that that indicated that there was a  need  and it was persuasive and believable and  truthful  in describing that need would be able to  energize this country  and get it pointed and off in the right  direction  you know there's a tendency for people  to say oh where are the great leaders  you look around and you're uncomfortable  with this person or uncomfortable with  that person  i spoke at the u.s army association back  in sometime in the 1980s and  i i titled the speech where are the  great leaders  and and i pointed out that throughout my  early life in the 1930s  people served in the military  and it was tough these these people  would be lieutenants  for seven eight years they were dragging  their wives and their kids and their  spouses around  from lousy dusty bases in one  city to another place in another state  day  we year after year they'd get  transferred and they'd go do that  no one knew who they were no one gave a  darn  the military was not respected  and and world war ii comes we're  attacked in japan  and lo and behold who was there  hap arnold was there  admiral leahy was there  admiral king was there general macarthur  was there  general patton was there  think of that they were there all the  time  and they're there today  and they'll be there when they're needed  even though  today we don't know their names maybe  but believe me they're there and this is  a great country  and and i have every confidence in the  world  that people who have the benefit of an  education in this school  this college and the people who support  it  are wise enough to know that that is a  value  that's important it seems to me that  that we're  it's understandable if we worry when we  see  things not going the way we might like  them but by golly at bedrock  this is a great country the people are  great people  and we're going to do what needs to be  done and thank you very much  he's good
 

